In Verilog, the correct usage of blocking and non-blocking  operators is crucial for accurately modeling hardware. Choosing the wrong operator leads to
  simulation-synthesis mismatches an race conditions
  (1). BLOCKING ASSIGNMENT:
  * Denoted with '='.
  * The assignment will be written in the always or initial block inside the behavioural modeling where the statements are executed or compiled by the computer
    sequentially.
  Example: {
             always
               begin
                 a=0;
                 b=1;
  Here the both execute sequentially at time t=0 . Where the left hand side variable a and b must be reg data type.
    Now we will talk about some stuff about time delay:
  (i).Inter Assignment time delay in Blocking Assignment:
   For example we take 
     always
            begin              #n is represented time delay in inter with which it represented before the variable
              #1 a=0;
              #2 b=1;
    First meaning of the inter delay is the signal is passed with n time delay into the variable to store before execution.
    Now in the blocking assignment the method is the compiler go to next part if and only if before statement is executed.

    By taking the above example for a =0 it takes 1ns(here i represent every thing in nano sec).For the execution of b=1 first you have to see the compiler will come 
    down to b after 1ns and when b=1 is 1+2= 3ns.
(ii).Intra Assignment time delay in Blocking Assignment:
    For example we take
      always@(*)
         begin
        a = #1 0;
        b = #2 1;
    the difference in the intra is the signal is not delay but the gate execution time or storing time in reg a has been delayed
    But we take the blocking assignment result is same that it block up to execution of a and a is executed 1ns and b will be executed in 3ns.

    (2).NON-BLOCKING ASSIGNMENT:
    * Denoted with '<=".
    * Unlike the blocking assignment here the statements will be executed parallely.
    Example:
    Example: {
             always@(*)
               begin
                 a<=0;
                 b<=1;
  }
    Both will execute parallely at t=0.
     (i) Inter assignment delay:
   For example we take 
     always
            begin              #n is represented time delay in inter with which it represented before the variable
              #1 a<=0;
              #2 b<=1;
     Here the signal started at t=1ns it will not wait for execution of a instead of it will do parallely the execution of b where time of execution of b is 
     1+2 = 3ns. and a is 1ns.
     (ii) Intra assignment delay:
   For example we take 
     always
            begin              #n is represented time delay in inter with which it represented before the assigning value.
               a<=#1 0;
               b<=#2 1;
     Here the signal started at t=0ns and the gate execution time is delayed by 1ns it will not wait for execution of a instead of it will do 
     parallely the execution of b where time of execution of b is  2ns and a is 1ns.

